Calc: cell formats are registered through a hash index (workbooks with thousands of formats open faster).

CellAttributeHelper::registerAndCheck() looked for an equal registered ScPatternAttr by scanning all
registered entries with the same cell style name and comparing hash codes. Workbooks with thousands of
distinct cell formats (one style, many fonts/fills/borders/number formats) register patterns for every
attribute run while loading, so loading was O(formats x runs): a synthetic 4000-format .xlsx spent
74 % of a 290 s load in that scan.

The helper now also keeps every registered entry in an unordered_multimap keyed by its hash code
(which includes the style name), and looks candidates up there. Each entry remembers the key it was
indexed under, so unregistering finds it again. The operations that change registered entries in place
(cell style deleted/created/renamed, all style sheets updated or turned into names, re-index) rebuild
the index; a rename now also drops the stale cached hash codes of the renamed entries.

